          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding remarks contrast the depth of her love with her inability or unwillingness to voice it extravagantly, reinforcing the avowal rather than weakening its truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Then poor Cordelia,\\nAnd yet not so; since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The immediately following modifier, “According to my bond,” makes this occurrence an affirmation and invocation of Cordelia’s existing filial obligation, not merely a report of feeling. Her next turn identifies the bond’s duties as reciprocating Lear’s parental care through obedience, love, and honour. It does not create a new promise or specify a new future undertaking, but it substantially positions her as bound by an enduring father–daughter relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, reasoned answer defining the proper measure of Cordelia’s love and duty, rather than a reflex-like affective discharge. Her statement that she cannot put her heart into speech further distinguishes inward feeling from spontaneous verbal release.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and affirmation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a measured corrective refusal to compete in Lear’s demand for maximal declarations. “No more nor less” sets a boundary against exaggeration, and Lear’s demand that she revise the answer confirms its resistant force in this exchange.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"How, how, Cordelia? Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most while linking their answers to their shares of his kingdom. After declining to produce an elaborate declaration, Cordelia answers Lear directly with the focal avowal, qualifies it by her filial bond, and refuses both exaggeration and diminution.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The occurrence invokes and affirms Cordelia’s pre-existing filial bond: because Lear begot, raised, and loved her, she presents herself as owing fitting reciprocal duties of obedience, love, and honour. This is not a newly created promise. Lear does not accept the measured affirmation as an adequate answer; he tells her to amend it and warns that her fortunes are at risk.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the supplied passage as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare’s King Lear; the title and scene text also identify it explicitly.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports Cordelia’s represented affection and invocation of filial duty, but it does not by itself determine every practical term or future duration of that duty. The assessment concerns this occurrence and Lear’s immediate response, without relying on omitted later events.\"}"
        }
